"""Days5–6: prove awkward paths and prepare a reproducible release."""
L(4,'1. Build a test strategy around the product promises','Choose pure, API, browser and container checks for the risks they can observe.',
 [('Test layers','Pure logic/config checks, real temporary-database API checks, browser journeys and actual container integration each reveal different failures.'),('Risk-based coverage','Prioritise broken ownership, lost work, invalid writes, session leaks and recovery. Counting checks is useful inventory, not proof of complete behaviour.')],
 'Start with the six stories and their failure consequences. A private-note leak matters even if every button looks correct. A failed save that clears a draft matters even if backend CRUD tests pass. A Docker image that cannot reach its database matters even if the in-process client works. Choose evidence at the boundary where each risk becomes visible.\n\nThe project’s tests own their data and fictional identities. API tests deny accidental live HTTP transport. The browser suite runs a real loopback server and deliberately simulates specific failures through request interception; its normal flows still use the real API. Container checks use actual PostgreSQL. Keep these distinctions in your test report instead of calling all of them unit tests.',
 r'''from lesson_support import run_checks
r=run_checks("def test_page_math():\n    assert max(0, ((6-1)//5)*5) == 5\n")
print("Passed:",r["passed"])
print("Failed:",r["failed"])''',
 'Map two risks to the layer that can actually observe them.',
 'matrix={"foreign note read":"API two-account test","draft after failed click":"real browser test","PostgreSQL restore":"container integration"}\nfor risk,layer in matrix.items():print(risk+":",layer)',
 'Read the documented evidence categories rather than inferring them from filenames.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint("Done evidence categories:",len(s["definition_of_done"]))',
 [('Can API tests alone prove a failed browser save preserves a textarea?','No','Yes','Only on localhost','Observe the actual browser interaction boundary.'),('Is a large test count the same as complete risk coverage?','No','Always','Only with containers','Check relevance and untested failure consequences.')],
 'Choose tests only by what is easiest to count.','Connect each important failure to a check at the right boundary.','The evidence should match the promise being made.')

L(4,'2. Test boundaries and unchanged-state invariants','Verify acceptance, rejection and persistence with independent observations.',
 [('Arrange / Act / Assert','Prepare known state, perform one meaningful action and inspect the observable result. Avoid tests that merely repeat the implementation’s formula.'),('Unchanged-state invariant','A rejected request must leave valid stored state intact. Read it back independently after the action.')],
 'An invalid request status is one assertion, not the whole behaviour. Start with a saved valid note, submit a malformed change and retrieve it again. Compare public content and verify the expected state remains. Use boundaries for title/body/page limits and cases for omitted, false, null and wrong-type inputs.\n\nPrefer independent expectations. If a page calculation is the thing under test, hard-code a few reviewed expected offsets instead of computing both sides with the same expression. If the query contract is under test, inspect actual rows and total. The supplied project’s API suite also verifies literal percent and underscore searches, ownership and session replay.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    before=c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).json()\n    r=c.patch("/v1/notes/1",headers=h,json={"body":"   "})\n    after=c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).json()\n    print("Rejected:",r.status_code==422)\n    print("Unchanged:",after==before)',
 'Check both the smallest supported page size and an invalid size.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    for limit in [1,0]:\n        print(limit,c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"limit":limit}).status_code)',
 'Verify one filtered row by content rather than only total.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    page=c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"q":"Validation"}).json()\n    print("Total:",page["total"])\n    print("Actual titles:",[n["title"] for n in page["items"]])',
 [('What strengthens an invalid-write test?','A later independent read shows unchanged data','Repeating only422','Reading the old Python variable','Inspect the persisted result after the attempted change.'),('Should expected values always be calculated with the same formula as the implementation?','No','Yes','Only for pagination','Independent reviewed expectations can catch the shared mistake.')],
 'Assert only that a function returned something truthy.','Assert the specific contract and its stored consequences.','A meaningful test can tell you which promise broke.')

L(4,'3. Test hostile inputs without confusing them with executable instructions','Check ownership and literal interpretation through the actual contract.',
 [('Adversarial input case','A deliberately awkward or malicious-looking request sent only to an owned test application to verify rejection or literal treatment.'),('Parameter binding and interpretation boundary','SQL values remain parameters; browser strings remain text. Correct SQL binding does not automatically solve browser script interpretation.')],
 'Use only the owned temporary app and fictional notes. Send a SQL-shaped search string and expect a literal search with no leaked rows. Send an attempted owner_id field and expect rejection. Try Ben’s token on Asha’s resource and verify denial. These tests exercise the application’s own boundaries rather than exploring somebody else’s system.\n\nKeep SQL and browser risks separate. Bound SQL prevents query values from becoming SQL syntax. textContent prevents a displayed string from becoming parsed HTML in this plain-text interface. A stored string can pass one layer safely and still need correct treatment at the next. The tests should say exactly which boundary they observed.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    page=c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"q":"\' OR 1=1 --"}).json()\n    print("Literal matches:",page["total"])\n    print("Owner total still:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h).json()["total"])',
 'Check a foreign patch and then retrieve the unchanged owner title.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,login\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.patch("/v1/notes/1",headers=login(c,"ben"),json={"title":"Not allowed"})\n    print("Foreign patch:",r.status_code)\n    print("Owner title:",c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).json()["title"])',
 'Verify literal underscore does not act as an all-matching wildcard.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Underscore matches:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"q":"_"}).json()["total"])',
 [('Does SQL parameter binding prove browser text cannot execute?','No','Yes','Only with an ORM','SQL interpretation and HTML interpretation are separate.'),('Where should these adversarial cases run?','Your owned disposable app','An unrelated public service','A stranger’s account','Keep verification within the authorised test boundary.')],
 'Assume one safe layer makes every later interpretation safe.','Test the independent SQL, permission and browser boundaries.','Security properties need evidence where interpretation occurs.')

L(4,'4. Exercise failures in the real browser','Verify draft preservation, partial-save retry and late-response isolation.',
 [('Browser interception','The test controls a stated HTTP response or delay to reproduce a failure deterministically. Name the simulated part instead of claiming a real outage occurred.'),('State-transition test','Verify what was visible before, during and after an action, including disabled controls and the preserved editor or cleared session.')],
 'Run browser_smoke.cjs with Node and Chrome. It starts a real loopback Python server and a fresh profile. Ordinary registration, note/tag writes, search, filters and pages hit the real database. For one failed write and one failed tag update it supplies a controlled503; for the logout race it holds an old read response and releases it afterward.\n\nRead the assertions: a failed ordinary write keeps the editor; a confirmed note plus failed tags retries the same identity; an old private response cannot repopulate signed-out cards. It also checks two accounts, text injection, paging recovery, refresh credentials and mobile themes. These are reproducible product checks, not evidence that a public host has been deployed.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path("browser_smoke.cjs").read_text()\nfor case in ["Failed write lost draft","Retry duplicated note","Old response repopulated signed-out UI"]:\n    print(case+":",case in s)',
 'Describe the distinction between a simulated error and a real normal flow.',
 'evidence={"normal CRUD":"real loopback API","one503":"controlled response","public HTTPS":"not checked here"}\nfor name,kind in evidence.items():print(name+":",kind)',
 'Write independent expected results for the three failure transitions.',
 'expected=["ordinary failed write retains draft","confirmed partial retry keeps note id","logout leaves no private cards"]\nfor item in expected:print(item)',
 [('Does a controlled503 prove an actual provider outage happened?','No','Yes','Only in Chrome','It proves the client response to that stated failure.'),('What should a delayed pre-logout read do after logout?','Leave private UI empty','Restore old notes','Automatically sign in again','The response belongs to an obsolete session state.')],
 'Test only the happy path and infer every interrupted flow works.','Reproduce failures and assert the exact state transitions.','Reliability is most visible when an ordinary operation is interrupted.',shell='python -m pytest -q\n# With Node24 and Chrome installed:\nnode browser_smoke.cjs')

L(4,'5. Polish accessibility and awkward interaction paths','Make labels, focus, confirmation and feedback usable together.',
 [('Live region and visible feedback','A role=status region announces changing outcomes politely. Text feedback complements colour and remains visible for review.'),('Native dialog and keyboard focus','A labelled modal confirmation supports browser focus handling. Put initial focus on the safe cancel option; return focus to a useful control after the action.')],
 'A good interface helps the learner recover. Label each input, retain visible keyboard focus and avoid using colour as the only result indicator. The dashboard uses a native delete dialog with a safe Keep note option. Form submission handles keyboard use as well as clicks. Buttons have useful touch sizes and controls wrap instead of forcing horizontal scrolling.\n\nCheck375px width in both themes, zoom and normal keyboard order. A headless no-overflow assertion is useful but does not certify all accessibility. Manually test a screen reader, high zoom, long content and the target phone too. A helpful error message should say what is known and what the user can do next, not merely “something happened”.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    html=c.get("/").text\n    print("Status region:",\'role="status"\' in html)\n    print("Named delete dialog:",\'aria-labelledby="deleteTitle"\' in html)\n    print("Skip link:","Skip to workspace" in html)',
 'Confirm the layout declares visible focus and a mobile breakpoint.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ncss=Path("orm_api/static/dashboard.css").read_text()\nprint("Focus rule:",":focus-visible" in css)\nprint("Mobile rule:","max-width:720px" in css)',
 'Create a manual accessibility checklist that complements automation.',
 'checks=["Tab through form and dialog","read errors without relying on colour","test high zoom and long text","check target screen reader"]\nprint("Manual checks:",len(checks))',
 [('Does no horizontal overflow certify all accessibility?','No','Yes','Only on mobile','It verifies one layout property, not every access need.'),('Which delete-dialog action should initially receive safe focus?','Keep note','Irreversible delete','A hidden input','Make the non-destructive choice easy to select.')],
 'Communicate errors only with a red border and remove focus outlines.','Use labelled controls, readable messages and visible keyboard focus.','Polish should reduce user uncertainty and effort.')

L(4,'6. Turn test results into a release gate','Review failed checks and known limitations before calling the build ready.',
 [('Release gate','A required check whose failure stops promotion. Keep tests, formatting and actual runtime evidence explicit rather than ignoring failing steps.'),('Coverage interpretation','Coverage reports execution, not whether assertions are good or every requirement was considered. Pair a coverage view with a risk checklist.')],
 'Run the supplied tests and inspect any failure before changing expected values. The project workflow runs isolated Python checks, lint/format, real browser journeys and actual Docker/PostgreSQL integration. It verifies the packaged frontend, ownership-related server behaviour, persistent data across API recreation and a backup restored into a separate database.\n\nRecord results for the source/artifact being reviewed. A green run from another version is not proof of new changes. Avoid a claim of “full test coverage” without a defined measure and its limitations. Public deployment and another-device HTTPS verification are still separate gates; the workflow does not buy a host or configure your domain.',
 r'''from lesson_support import run_checks
r=run_checks("def test_known_boundary():\n    assert 0 < 1 <= 50\ndef test_failure_is_reported():\n    assert False\n")
print("Passed:",r["passed"])
print("Failed:",r["failed"])
print("Gate open:",r["exit_code"]==0)''',
 'Decide whether a release is eligible from explicit observed gates.',
 'gates={"API":True,"browser":True,"containers":False}\nprint("Promote:",all(gates.values()))\nprint("Investigate:",[k for k,v in gates.items() if not v])',
 'Keep a coverage percentage separate from unmet product evidence.',
 'report={"line_coverage":92,"screen_reader_checked":False}\nprint("Coverage:",report["line_coverage"])\nprint("All access needs proven:",report["screen_reader_checked"])',
 [('Does high line coverage prove every requirement works?','No','Always','Only at100%','Executed lines and meaningful behavioural evidence differ.'),('Should a failed required check be ignored to finish the day?','No','Yes','Only for a demo','Investigate the failure or state the blocker honestly.')],
 'Mark the release ready because most checks passed on an older commit.','Review required evidence for the actual candidate and keep missing checks pending.','A release gate is a decision backed by specific evidence.')

L(5,'1. Make a fresh setup reproducible','Write instructions another learner can follow without your local state.',
 [('Reproducible handoff','A clean folder, pinned dependencies, explicit commands, known startup URL and stated runtime assumptions.'),('Environment-specific secret','Configuration supplied privately to a selected runtime, not committed source. Fictional example values are not a credential-provisioning strategy.')],
 'Begin with the README, not with your current terminal history. Create an environment, install the pinned tools, run tests and start python app.py. Visit the root dashboard and the optional /docs explorer. The local teaching server uses loopback and its own SQLite instance; it is not a public service merely because it runs.\n\nExplain where commands run and how to stop the server. On Windows, the README uses separate PowerShell lines; activation is a convenience, so the environment’s Python executable can be called directly when activation is unavailable. Installing packages initially needs network access. Reading the Study Hub lessons afterward remains offline; running this separate database app needs its own server.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nrequired=["README.md","spec.json","requirements-dev.txt","app.py","server.py","browser_smoke.cjs"]\nprint("Handoff files:",all(Path(name).is_file() for name in required))',
 'Confirm the entry pages are documented rather than assumed.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path("README.md").read_text()\nprint("Local dashboard URL:","http://127.0.0.1:8002/" in s)\nprint("API explorer route:","/docs" in s)',
 'Reject a missing production signing configuration through the actual settings contract.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ntry:\n    Settings.from_env({"APP_ENV":"production"})\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Missing production signing secret rejected")',
 [('Does a clean handoff depend on your private instance directory?','No','Yes','Only for login','Provide setup and fresh storage instead of personal runtime state.'),('Does running on loopback create a public deployment?','No','Yes','Only with FastAPI','An external runtime and routing are separate.')],
 'Write “run it normally” and rely on your cached tools and accounts.','Document exact setup, entry URLs, assumptions and fresh practice data.','A reproducible project can be reviewed independently.',shell='python -m venv .venv\n# Activate the environment or call its Python directly.\npython -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt\npython -m pytest -q\npython app.py\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:8002/')

L(5,'2. Package the same product with Docker and PostgreSQL','Verify that the real frontend ships with the runtime, not only the Python routes.',
 [('Artifact completeness','The image must include the actual HTML, CSS and JavaScript as well as the Python API, migrations and runtime dependencies.'),('Runtime parity check','Exercise the important contract on the packaged target database. A successful local SQLite request alone does not verify PostgreSQL portability or container networking.')],
 'The supplied Dockerfile copies orm_api, including the dashboard assets, into a non-root runtime. Compose starts PostgreSQL, one migration job and the API. The API joins a normal frontend bridge for its loopback-published HTTP port and an internal backend for database access; the database has no published host port.\n\nRun the container check only on a fresh disposable stack because it creates fixed fictional data and a restore database. It verifies the packaged page/assets, actual PostgreSQL auth/CRUD/search/filter, restricted runtime authority, API recreation persistence, outage recovery and a separate restore. Ordinary stopping keeps your own volume; removal is restricted to disposable owned test state.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\nprint("Project:",c["name"])\nprint("Published:",c["services"]["api"]["ports"])\nprint("DB host publication:",c["services"]["db"].get("ports",[]))',
 'Check that the Docker copy includes the directory holding all three dashboard files.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nprint("Runtime package copied:","COPY orm_api ./orm_api" in Path("Dockerfile").read_text())\nprint("Assets present:",all((Path("orm_api/static")/name).exists() for name in ["dashboard.html","dashboard.css","dashboard.js"]))',
 'Confirm migration and runtime identities differ in the actual Compose source.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ns=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nprint("Different roles:",s["api"]["environment"]["DB_USER"]!=s["migrate"]["environment"]["DB_USER"])',
 [('Can a green SQLite check alone prove the container/PostgreSQL workflow?','No','Yes','Only with SQLAlchemy','Verify the actual packaged runtime boundary.'),('Should a runtime image omit the new dashboard assets?','No','Yes','Only behind a proxy','The complete user workflow depends on those files.')],
 'Ship the Python API but forget the actual interface files.','Build and test the complete artifact on its target database/runtime.','Packaging is part of the full-stack user journey.',shell='python ops.py init-secrets\ndocker compose config --quiet\ndocker compose up --build -d --wait api\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:8043/\n# Fresh disposable stack only: python container_smoke.py')

L(5,'3. Plan a public release and verify it from another device','Separate repository publication, server deployment and external HTTPS evidence.',
 [('Release record','The chosen source revision, artifact identity, schema head, configuration version, checks and recovery plan for a deployment candidate.'),('External smoke verification','A short real workflow through the actual HTTPS hostname from another network/device, including denied actions and persistence.')],
 'Follow the README’s ordered checklist on a provider account and budget you choose. Configure a Docker web service rooted at docs/python-week43 and a fresh managed PostgreSQL database. Supply runtime secrets privately, use verified database TLS, run one controlled migration with migration credentials, then start the API with its restricted role.\n\nVerify the provider hostname and certificate before custom DNS. Check the actual dashboard, register/login, save and retrieve notes, and prove another account cannot access them. Do this from your phone or another network. A local address on a screenshot does not establish public reachability. Keep unperformed external checks pending and avoid creating resources merely to make the milestone look finished.',
 'release={"source_revision":"record exact commit","schema":"w43_003","local_checks":"record result","public_https_phone_flow":"pending"}\nfor name,value in release.items():print(name+":",value)',
 'Gate public promotion on the complete stated evidence.',
 'checks={"HTTPS verified":True,"owner flow":True,"other owner denied":True,"restore rehearsed":False}\nprint("Promote:",all(checks.values()))',
 'Confirm the provider-port precedence in the real configuration class.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ns=Settings.from_env({"PORT":"9080","API_PORT":"8002"})\nprint("Selected listening port:",s.port)',
 [('Does publishing the repository deploy its Python process?','No','Always','Only with Pages','Source hosting and an application runtime are different.'),('When can the public phone flow be marked passed?','After observing it at the real HTTPS hostname','After local TestClient passes','After writing a checklist','The evidence must cross the stated external boundary.')],
 'Call a source-code publication a deployed full-stack service.','Verify the actual runtime, HTTPS route and external user flow.','Deployment claims should name what was really observed.')

L(5,'4. Document architecture and capture honest portfolio evidence','Explain decisions and provide usable setup, screenshots and limitations.',
 [('Architecture overview','A concise map of components, state ownership, trust boundaries and transaction flow. It should explain why the parts exist.'),('Portfolio case study','A problem, scoped solution, decisions, evidence and limitations. Screenshots support the story but do not replace runnable source or tests.')],
 'Write the story for another learner: what problem Study Notes solves, how to run it, what a saved note crosses and where identity is enforced. Include a plain architecture sketch and explain the two-stage note/tag save and memory-only session. Those decisions are more useful than a long list of technology names.\n\nCapture real fictional-data screenshots without tokens or passwords. The browser suite saves desktop/mobile images to the operating system’s temporary folder; public staging screenshots are another stage. Record the current source and observed tests. List the simultaneous-edit, idempotency, recovery and operational gaps rather than hiding them behind a polished hero image.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint("Case study name:",s["name"])\nprint("Known deferred capabilities:",len(s["out_of_scope"]))',
 'Trace the state owners in an architecture summary.',
 'flow=[("editor","browser memory"),("owned note","database"),("schema history","Alembic"),("runtime secret","private environment")]\nfor item,owner in flow:print(item+":",owner)',
 'Find the README sections someone reviewing the project needs.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path("README.md").read_text()\nfor heading in ["## Run locally","## Architecture","## Portfolio evidence"]:\n    print(heading,heading in s)',
 [('Can a screenshot replace a reproducible setup?','No','Always','Only for a portfolio','Provide commands, source and the actual evidence boundaries.'),('Should the case study hide known partial-write behaviour?','No','Yes','Only for reviewers','Explaining trade-offs shows understanding and protects users.')],
 'List framework names and claim every feature is production-ready.','Explain the actual problem, architecture, evidence and remaining work.','A strong portfolio piece can be understood and reproduced.')

L(5,'5. Prepare recovery, cost and incident ownership','Keep the data source intact while rehearsing a separate restore.',
 [('Recovery rehearsal','Restore a protected backup into a different owned target and verify representative data, schema and permissions. File existence alone is not recovery evidence.'),('Operational owner and budget policy','Someone must respond to failures, verify alerts, maintain secrets/dependencies and review total resource costs. An alert is not automatically a hard spending cap.')],
 'Use the Week43 backup commands against only this lab’s database. The dump is made inside the container and copied as a file, avoiding older PowerShell binary redirection pitfalls. Restore into a separate owned database and validate counts and schema. If that name already exists, choose another instead of dropping unknown data.\n\nDatabase recovery is separate from returning to an older application image. Check schema compatibility, retain a previous artifact and keep a reviewed incident path. Protect backup copies independently with appropriate access, encryption and retention. For a public service, configure real external uptime/alert delivery and a clear owner; the project does not create a monitoring subscription for you.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path("container_smoke.py").read_text()\nprint("Separate restore target used:","studyhub_restore" in s)\nprint("Correct schema checked:","w43_003" in s)\nprint("Drops source database:","DROP DATABASE" in s)',
 'Distinguish code rollback from restoring application data.',
 'recovery={"code":"compatible previous artifact","data":"verified separate restore","configuration":"private recovery procedure"}\nfor part,plan in recovery.items():print(part+":",plan)',
 'Record an unresolved operations gate explicitly.',
 'operations={"alert owner chosen":True,"notification tested":False,"budget policy verified":False}\nprint("Still required:",[name for name,done in operations.items() if not done])',
 [('Does the presence of a dump prove it can restore?','No','Yes','Only with PostgreSQL','Restore and validate it independently.'),('Can replacing the app image alone undo every schema/data change?','No','Always','Only on a managed host','Code compatibility and data recovery require separate plans.')],
 'Restore over the only source database before validating the backup.','Rehearse on a separate owned target and preserve the source.','Recovery should reduce uncertainty without creating avoidable data loss.')

L(5,'6. Milestone: a complete and explainable Study Notes app','Demonstrate the finished learning slice and state the remaining external release work.',
 [('Milestone demonstration','Show requirements through backend rules, browser journeys, tests, runtime persistence and documentation. Explain each major boundary in your own words.'),('Honest completion record','Local, browser, container and public-host outcomes are separate facts. A complete lesson package can still leave your chosen public deployment exercise pending.')],
 'Run the app and demonstrate the six stories. Register a fictional account, create/tag/edit a note, search/filter/page, complete it, cancel and confirm deletion, refresh and sign in again. Show another account’s denial. Explain what happens when a write fails, when tags fail after a confirmed save and when an old search finishes after logout.\n\nThen run the supplied checks and read the README’s release and recovery plan. If you choose to host publicly, complete the separate HTTPS/phone, monitoring and budget gates and record actual evidence. Do not rush ahead because this is called a one-week sprint. The goal is a product you can operate, explain, test and improve, while the rest of Study Hub and your earlier progress remain independent.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Dashboard:",c.get("/").status_code)\n    print("Owner notes:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h).json()["total"])\n    print("Anonymous denied:",c.get("/v1/notes").status_code)\n    print("Ready:",c.get("/health/ready").status_code)',
 'Demonstrate a complete owned create/read/complete/delete lifecycle.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"My explanation","body":"I can trace the whole flow"})\n    url=r.headers["Location"]\n    print("Created:",r.status_code)\n    print("Completed:",c.patch(url,headers=h,json={"completed":True}).json()["completed"])\n    print("Deleted:",c.delete(url,headers=h).status_code)',
 'Write a truthful final evidence record for your own run.',
 'evidence={"I ran the app":"record result","I explained ownership":"record result","I verified public phone HTTPS":"pending until performed"}\nfor check,result in evidence.items():print(check+":",result)',
 [('What is the sprint’s learning goal?','An app you can explain, verify and operate','A screenshot alone','As many tools as possible','The complete journey matters more than a technology list.'),('Should you skip unfinished understanding to fit six calendar days?','No','Yes','Only before deployment','Split the work into more study sessions when needed.')],
 'Call the sprint complete because the first happy-path screenshot looks finished.','Demonstrate the whole scoped workflow and record real evidence at each boundary.','A useful full-stack project is understandable, testable and honest about its limits.',shell='python -m pytest -q\npython -m ruff check .\npython -m ruff format --check .\nnode browser_smoke.cjs\npython app.py\n# Dashboard: http://127.0.0.1:8002/\n# Follow README for the separate Docker/public-host exercises.')
